53
00:03:33,949 --> 00:03:36,990
So, that's pretty interesting that a language that we're currently talking about or like,

54
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,870
"Is this the new language in safety-critical?" comes out of a space.

55
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:47,111
that is pretty far from safety-critical, but mindset-wise is pretty close. Mhm.

56
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:53,870
Yeah, and I think that sometimes we realize that the systems are safety-critical when something bad happens.

57
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:58,590
You know, like cloud providers that have some kind of loss of service for whatever reason.

58
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:00,990
Or I think some time ago happened even to Google.

59
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,710
I think Gmail issues, things.

60
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:09,310
So, and all of a sudden entire businesses cannot really work anymore. Yeah, that was a memory safety issue.

61
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:13,470
It was a null pointer dereference that they actually actively ran into.

62
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:15,151
The retro about that is quite clear about this. So.

63
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:19,990
Yeah, you are smiling because this is like it wouldn't have happened with the rust, I guess.

64
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,030
I'm joking.

65
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:29,390
In a way, yes. There's other bugs of that scale that you can build in rust, but rust is there to help with that.

66
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:34,750
So, I think one of the things that legitimizes I don't need to legitimize rust, but.

67
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:40,430
that is interesting about this whole move is rust is not alone in being a new memory safe.

68
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:42,190
systems programming language.

69
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:47,750
For example, Apple has developed Swift, which is pretty much in the same generation of programming languages.

70
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:49,790
