71
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:51,430
We know it better.

72
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:54,230
It is much more a generation of new software development.

73
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:59,470
that also then invests into new base tooling because it has new needs.

74
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:01,550
Yeah, yeah.

75
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:03,830
Can I ask you, what are the criteria to decide.

76
00:05:03,830 --> 00:05:08,909
that a mission or a safety critical system is actually good enough for its purpose?

77
00:05:08,919 --> 00:05:11,909
This is one of the things where the safety critical community has an edge.

78
00:05:11,919 --> 00:05:17,070
because it has standards and documents and aside from these documents, forums.

79
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:21,190
where this is constantly being discussed.

80
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:23,710
And that is something that we don't really see in,

81
00:05:23,710 --> 00:05:26,950
for example, like before I had fair systems, I was actually in data center operations.

82
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:28,710
So, I have quite a bit of insight there.

83
00:05:28,710 --> 00:05:32,310
So, while there are background groups that talk about these issues,

84
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:39,310
there's nothing as structured as here's an industry consortium that wants to fix these and these issues.

85
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:41,030
and talk about how our programming practice looks like.

86
00:05:41,030 --> 00:05:46,990
So, things like Misra, things like all the standards bodies in the ISO.

87
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:52,590
that decide about the IEC 61508 for industry or the ISO 26262 for automotive.

88
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:56,430
Like all these forums or the DO-178 for avionics.

89
00:05:56,430 --> 00:06:01,430
where you have written down standard engineering practice of what is expected.

90
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:04,510
and what is recommended that just doesn't exist in.

91
00:06:04,510 --> 00:06:06,870
that mission critical space.

92
00:06:06,870 --> 00:06:08,790
So, I think a little bit there the label helps.

93
00:06:08,790 --> 00:06:12,150
if it's safety critical or something where one of those standards is in play.

94
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:15,870
So, can you give us an example, you know, a few things, you know,

95
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:19,630
some of these criteria or some examples of what these documents specify?

96
00:06:19,630 --> 00:06:21,590
Just a small one to give people an idea of what we look at.

97
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:29,070
The one I love is that the ISO 26262, for example, says practice on all levels of safety.

98
00:06:29,070 --> 00:06:34,430
So, it has these ASIL A, B, C, D for different levels of toughness essentially.

99
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:38,351
It says, "If available, use a statically typed programming language." That is one of the criteria.

100
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:42,390
And it's actually highly recommended on all levels.

101
00:06:42,390 --> 00:06:45,270
Which I sometimes use as a joke when people ask like, "Should we use Rust in automotive?"

102
00:06:45,270 --> 00:06:47,390
And I say, "Yes, your standard says so."

103
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:54,430
But there's other things like, for example, practice restricted use of pointers.

104
00:06:54,430 --> 00:06:56,270
It says like, "Don't just hand pointers around everywhere.

105
00:06:56,270 --> 00:07:00,310
If you can pass things by value, pass them by value because that's easier."

106
00:07:00,310 --> 00:07:02,270
That's one of those things that could be in a standard.

107
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:08,510
or even on certain levels to assess the quality of your test suite,

108
00:07:08,510 --> 00:07:12,150
please use code coverage of different kinds of forms.

109
00:07:12,150 --> 00:07:14,870
Like, for example, please use statement coverage.

110
00:07:14,870 --> 00:07:20,790
Is, for example, the one that the automotive standard says for ASIL B, so roughly mid-level.

111
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:26,470
While it says for ASIL D, use multiple condition and decision coverage, MCDC.

112
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:28,230
So, those are things that it mandates.

113
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:32,990
So, it mandates and recommends activities.

114
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:38,230
One important thing is that all of those activities can be argued at a level where you say,

115
00:07:38,230 --> 00:07:40,950
"I do this activity.

116
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:43,150
I don't do activity A, but I don't do activity B.

117
00:07:43,150 --> 00:07:49,070
because I can give you an argument why in my software it actually doesn't improve quality that much.

118
00:07:49,070 --> 00:07:51,110
and will actually focus on making the first one really thorough."

119
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:54,630
That is a debate you then have to have with your assessor.

120
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:57,230
And this is the other practice where I would differentiate safety-critical from mission-critical,

121
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:04,230
where this practice of your software is being assessed by an independent party is much more structured.

122
00:08:04,230 --> 00:08:11,391
While in mission-critical, particularly in cybersecurity, it is just I'm doing air quotes here good practice, but it's also.

123
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:15,950
So, for example, we do work on cryptographic code.

124
00:08:15,950 --> 00:08:20,550
We have worked, for example, on Rust TLS, which means we will never assess this code.

125
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:22,350
We will never claim that this code is good.

126
00:08:22,350 --> 00:08:25,430
That is for a third party to assess, and I think this is good practice.

127
00:08:25,430 --> 00:08:31,430
This interplay between the implementer is not the validator, I think is.

128
00:08:31,430 --> 00:08:35,070
And that's completely independent of programming languages.

129
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:39,029
So, do I understand correctly that this basically a set of recommendations at various levels,

130
00:08:39,029 --> 00:08:44,431
even to the nitty-gritty details of everyday coding, up to that? Yeah.

131
00:08:44,431 --> 00:08:47,910
And then any decisions that are taken maybe outside the recommendations,

132
00:08:47,910 --> 00:08:53,310
what you do are actually explicitly and documented decisions, so it's not by random chance that things happen.

133
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,630
Exactly.

134
00:08:55,630 --> 00:08:57,230
And it's totally fine to do things that are outside of the standard.

135
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:02,110
You can still go and say, we have an activity that is not covered in the standard,

136
00:09:02,110 --> 00:09:04,150
but we still think it increases software quality.

137
00:09:04,150 --> 00:09:10,671
In that case, that's a longer argument to make because you have to say it is of utility, but that's stuff.

138
00:09:10,671 --> 00:09:14,110
Like, the standards are not prescriptive.

139
00:09:14,110 --> 00:09:16,030
They are not saying like you should do this.

140
00:09:16,030 --> 00:09:19,270
The good thing I find about the safety standards is that they say,

141
00:09:19,270 --> 00:09:22,750
this is a very good recommendation of what you should do.

142
00:09:22,750 --> 00:09:24,390
If you're diverging from it, we need to have a conversation.

143
00:09:24,390 --> 00:09:28,350
and someone needs to trust that your divergence is good and makes sense.

144
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:32,910
And then how do you decide what to apply and what to apply? So, I'll give you an example.

145
00:09:32,910 --> 00:09:37,831
So, in a safety-critical system, of course, means that lives might be at risk. Mhm.

146
00:09:37,831 --> 00:09:43,710
How do you decide it could be acceptable to risk some lives in some circumstances?

147
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:45,670
So, how is this, you know, the criteria?

148
00:09:45,670 --> 00:09:47,430
So, it's just try to understand, you know,

149
00:09:47,430 --> 00:09:53,870
when people go down and say, "Okay, according to what you have done, this is a good system."

150
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:56,070
Because of course you cannot really eliminate the risk entirely.

151
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:58,630
Yeah. This is a little bit out of my depth.

152
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:05,590
because I am very much on the front of the chain in providing the tools for this.

153
00:10:05,590 --> 00:10:07,550
I've never been in the position where I would have to argue that.

154
00:10:07,550 --> 00:10:09,590
I know that these assessments are being made.

155
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:17,230
and I know that for example the avionics standards are so strict because disaster is usually pretty big.

156
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:19,390
While if you're talking about a car,

157
00:10:19,390 --> 00:10:21,990
that that level I can speak like.

158
00:10:21,990 --> 00:10:25,310
that just the system and the system complexity is just a completely different category.

159
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:27,550
Okay.

160
00:10:27,550 --> 00:10:29,870
If a plane falls from the sky, it's a massive massive disaster.

161
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:35,750
Automotive has the problem that billions and billions of cars drive every day.

162
00:10:35,750 --> 00:10:39,750
and they want to drive numbers down and accidents happen daily.

163
00:10:39,750 --> 00:10:42,230
That's a fact of life.

164
00:10:42,230 --> 00:10:45,870
But nowadays we're talking in Germany about thousands of accidents a year.

165
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:50,390
There were times where we were talking about 10,000s of accidents a year.

166
00:10:50,390 --> 00:10:51,950
So, bringing that number down and further.

167
00:10:51,950 --> 00:10:55,230
and further and further and making sure.

168
00:10:55,230 --> 00:10:58,150
that to also mechanical and ways and it's not just about the software.

169
00:10:58,150 --> 00:11:00,190
It's also how the car is built.

170
00:11:00,190 --> 00:11:02,351
It's about the system, the entire system. You can't escape the system.

171
00:11:02,351 --> 00:11:07,751
I have I had a very nice conversation at the Safety Critical Club the last time I was. there.

172
00:11:07,751 --> 00:11:11,990
where someone said, "Safety in the end is about dealing with what's real."

173
00:11:11,990 --> 00:11:17,030
Like you can't escape reality and you usually have a physical system in place.

174
00:11:17,030 --> 00:11:19,110
and the reality is car accidents exist.

175
00:11:19,110 --> 00:11:22,310
but there's a lot of people out there both on the software side.

176
00:11:22,310 --> 00:11:24,310
and the hardware side to make sure that people don't get harmed in them.

177
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:26,910
Yeah.

178
00:11:26,910 --> 00:11:31,471
Now, why do the tool chains for the critical systems need to be certified? What is the reason for that?

179
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:33,710
So, the general term is being qualified.

180
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:39,630
because the tool chain doesn't doesn't end up like your compiler doesn't run on an engine.

181
00:11:39,630 --> 00:11:41,430
The compiler runs in your build system.

182
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:46,310
Quite simply because compiler bugs can immediately lead to program misbehavior.

183
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:51,750
So, they're in the direct chain of the coder.

184
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:57,870
I write something into my text editor and this is the program that runs. And a lot of bugs are pretty much immediately obvious.

185
00:11:57,870 --> 00:12:00,472
That's the good ones. I run my tests. This is buggy.

186
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:06,190
That's fine. And then the program never gets deployed.

187
00:12:06,190 --> 00:12:09,470
The problem is compilers have the power to generate our code.

188
00:12:09,470 --> 00:12:14,790
and that also means any bugs in the code generation can lead to hard to trace down bugs.

189
00:12:14,790 --> 00:12:16,710
that come down the line that may be triggered later on.

190
00:12:16,710 --> 00:12:21,030
And this is why we assess tool chains.

191
00:12:21,030 --> 00:12:25,510
So, the joking summary if I have to explain ferrocene.

192
00:12:25,510 --> 00:12:29,150
and the whole subject of compiler qualification to people in the Rust project.

193
00:12:29,150 --> 00:12:33,990
who are away from safety critical is they're saying like, "What are you doing? The Rust compiler is already high quality."

194
00:12:33,990 --> 00:12:37,430
And I'm saying like, "Yeah, but what's your argument that it is high quality?

195
00:12:37,430 --> 00:12:39,350
Is it we haven't found bugs for a couple of weeks.

196
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:46,550
or is it we are sure that for example, this feature has an appropriate amount of tests.

197
00:12:46,550 --> 00:12:48,470
that we are reasonably sure that it is well tested."

198
00:12:48,470 --> 00:12:50,030
And that's basically the qualification work.

199
00:12:50,030 --> 00:12:53,271
You say. Mhm.

200
00:12:53,271 --> 00:12:55,470
the tool has the following feature.

201
00:12:55,470 --> 00:12:58,070
For example, it compiles a main function and if you put in print.

202
00:12:58,070 --> 00:13:06,631
that out hello, the output is a certain kind of binary program on a certain kind of architecture. that outputs hello world.

203
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:12,430
Pretty trivial example, but we make sure that this compilation process works well all over over time.

204
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:16,230
Mhm. And so, when you have this qualification process,

205
00:13:16,230 --> 00:13:20,630
is it done for a specific version of the compiler on a specific operating system on a specific.

206
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:23,271
architecture? Yes.

207
00:13:23,271 --> 00:13:28,030
Are all versions kind of locked down for the qualification process.

208
00:13:28,030 --> 00:13:30,470
or there is a bit of flexibility there.

209
00:13:30,470 --> 00:13:36,030
So, we generally release every 3 months, so every quarter.

210
00:13:36,030 --> 00:13:41,510
And we release an appropriate Rust compiler version that is currently new.

211
00:13:41,510 --> 00:13:46,830
We take a few 1 to 2 months to further validate the compiler downstream.

212
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:48,830
That means validating it on, as you say,

213
00:13:48,830 --> 00:13:51,150
we need to validate it on all the targets.

214
00:13:51,150 --> 00:13:52,990
that are supported that the main Rust project does not support.

215
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,470
So, we're running the tests there.

216
00:13:55,470 --> 00:14:00,350
We're seeing if exactly that thing is like we have for the whole Ferrous Systems project,

217
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:03,990
we have written a language specification for the Rust language, so.

218
00:14:03,990 --> 00:14:05,990
that we can say, "This is a feature of the language.

219
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,710
These are the tests for it."

220
00:14:07,710 --> 00:14:14,270
This specification, by the way, has recently been accepted off the Rust project as the Rust specification,

221
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:18,190
which means the argument, "Rust doesn't have a specification." now goes away.

222
00:14:18,190 --> 00:14:20,870
That was a decade-long argument that we had.

223
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:23,710
Rust now has a specification.

224
00:14:23,710 --> 00:14:25,510
So, a major trunk of that work is making sure.

225
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:31,231
that every piece in that specification is well tested. That's about 1/2.

226
00:14:31,231 --> 00:14:37,430
And the reason why we do this is to make sure that engineers that use the tool,

227
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:41,990
and they see a tool behavior, they can go to the specification.

228
00:14:41,990 --> 00:14:44,670
and say, "Does this behave like it's written down?"

