00:23:03,071 --> 00:23:08,990
So first of all, maybe a very brief overview of Rust with a focus on what you think.

364
00:23:08,990 --> 00:23:12,390
makes it suitable for mission and safety-critical systems.

365
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:14,150
Okay.

366
00:23:14,150 --> 00:23:15,710
So, Rust is a new systems programming language.

367
00:23:15,710 --> 00:23:21,390
that as its hallmark feature is memory safe and memory safe in the face of concurrency.

368
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:24,150
That's its two big strong points.

369
00:23:24,150 --> 00:23:28,150
It comes out of the language family of the ML families for those.

370
00:23:28,150 --> 00:23:30,590
that are interested in those bits.

371
00:23:30,590 --> 00:23:35,110
but it looks a lot like a standard general standard systems programming language.

372
00:23:35,120 --> 00:23:39,030
Comes, as I said, out of Mozilla research.

373
00:23:39,030 --> 00:23:43,070
and it was an approach to do better than C++ that which they were currently using.

374
00:23:43,070 --> 00:23:47,070
and fix a lot of the issues they were seeing on the Firefox code base.

375
00:23:47,070 --> 00:23:51,350
Though, the best comment I ever heard about that was if Rust is a criticism of C++,

376
00:23:51,350 --> 00:23:53,430
it comes from a place of love.

377
00:23:53,430 --> 00:23:55,710
So, it's built by people who are very pragmatic programmers.

378
00:23:55,710 --> 00:23:58,470
So, it has a lot of just around that.

379
00:23:58,470 --> 00:24:02,990
It comes with two very big concepts to ensure that, the concept of memory ownership.

380
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:11,270
It is always clear in a Rust program which part of the program currently owns a resource,

381
00:24:11,270 --> 00:24:14,390
generally memory, but also sometimes, for example, a lock or something else.

382
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:18,110
And then, on top of that, it has a feature that is called borrowing,

383
00:24:18,110 --> 00:24:24,030
which makes it safe to refer to other items in memory, usually through references.

384
00:24:24,040 --> 00:24:31,150
And these references carry what people often sometimes heard about this idea of lifetime.

385
00:24:31,150 --> 00:24:36,830
So, the Rust compiler very much tracks when data enters your program,

386
00:24:36,830 --> 00:24:39,350
when it exits the program, and then it tries to make sure.

387
00:24:39,350 --> 00:24:45,510
that every reference that's given out to that data is always legal and always dereferenceable.

