00:25:17,830 --> 00:25:21,230
You have that ownership thing, you have that borrowing thing,

397
00:25:21,230 --> 00:25:25,431
and otherwise it boils down to what I usually call a data structures and functions language, similar to C.

398
00:25:25,440 --> 00:25:31,630
So, Rust has two big primitives, data structures that you put in memory, functions you call on them.

399
00:25:31,640 --> 00:25:37,590
So, in the end it becomes very traceable what actually happens in runtime.

400
00:25:37,600 --> 00:25:41,990
The other thing is all of those checks happen at compile time.

401
00:25:41,990 --> 00:25:48,590
That means the Rust programming language does not have a runtime. It just executes code on the processor,

402
00:25:48,590 --> 00:25:52,790
which makes it really feasible to be a language that is put on other things with active behavior,

403
00:25:52,800 --> 00:25:56,350
saying operating system. or something like that.

404
00:25:56,350 --> 00:25:58,390
So, you don't have the situation where you have a.

405
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:05,830
Okay, I have the kernel, which is essentially a runtime system, and then I have, for example.

406
00:26:05,830 --> 00:26:08,350
if I take a language like Go,

407
00:26:08,350 --> 00:26:12,351
Go has this whole thing with an event reactor and an IO system on top of it, and I need to validate all.

