468
00:30:02,231 --> 00:30:07,310
The Rust compiler currently does not annotate V tables with their jump targets.

469
00:30:07,310 --> 00:30:12,550
So, virtual function tables, a way to dispatch, to do dynamic dispatch.

470
00:30:12,550 --> 00:30:15,310
So, you get this function table handed,

471
00:30:15,310 --> 00:30:18,910
and there's basically a pointer to data in memory,

472
00:30:18,910 --> 00:30:20,710
and a pointer to a virtual function table that are functions.

473
00:30:20,710 --> 00:30:23,110
that you can call on this object.

474
00:30:23,110 --> 00:30:30,030
And the Rust compiler does not currently properly encode all possible jump targets,

475
00:30:30,030 --> 00:30:31,830
which is clang, for example, does.

476
00:30:31,830 --> 00:30:34,070
Because even though this is a dynamic call,

477
00:30:34,070 --> 00:30:37,070
you can actually during compilation say, "These are all the possible jump targets."

478
00:30:37,070 --> 00:30:39,790
This is something that people want for cybersecurity,

479
00:30:39,790 --> 00:30:43,870
but also for safety, because then they can analyze all the possible calls that can happen here.

480
00:30:43,870 --> 00:30:45,710
and can make a performance assessment.

481
00:30:45,710 --> 00:30:51,791
And because that item is currently not emitted by the compiler, tools will say like, "Okay." And they.

482
00:30:51,791 --> 00:30:53,830
And the usual stance of the tool vendor is,

483
00:30:53,830 --> 00:30:58,550
"We'll fix it if it becomes an actual problem to a big customer."

484
00:30:58,550 --> 00:31:04,070
And most customers are currently like, "Okay, we can work around this because the program is so tiny.

485
00:31:04,070 --> 00:31:06,470
We just say this this and this is the jump target by code reading."

486
00:31:06,470 --> 00:31:08,470
And say to yourself, "We've done it manually."

