00:57:24,230 --> 00:57:25,910
if you have a project to offer.

903
00:57:25,910 --> 00:57:28,310
That's something that we've seen.

904
00:57:28,310 --> 00:57:33,630
The biggest mistake that we've seen is actually in hiring is a quite trivial one is expecting.

905
00:57:33,630 --> 00:57:36,870
too much Rust experience of potential hires.

906
00:57:36,870 --> 00:57:41,670
Because I know people in the Rust community who are extremely knowledgeable in the language,

907
00:57:41,670 --> 00:57:43,390
extremely knowledgeable in putting in of projects, but.

908
00:57:43,390 --> 00:57:47,750
who have never worked Rust on a job because they are C++ developers somewhere.

909
00:57:47,760 --> 00:57:54,470
Yeah, okay. And they often fall through screening because it says not 5 years of professional Rust experience,

910
00:57:54,470 --> 00:57:58,270
but they have 30 years of professional C++ programming experience.

911
00:57:58,280 --> 00:58:02,030
this is one of the unfortunate things with all development jobs.

912
00:58:02,030 --> 00:58:07,470
Often companies look for specific language stuff,

913
00:58:07,470 --> 00:58:11,470
when in fact experienced programmers can pick up the specific skills very, very quickly.

914
00:58:11,480 --> 00:58:14,310
But the job market, I think, is mature.

915
00:58:14,320 --> 00:58:18,990
and my indicators for a mature market is people move jobs.

916
00:58:18,990 --> 00:58:21,310
Like people move away from Rust jobs into Rust jobs.

917
00:58:21,310 --> 00:58:25,150
I think that's my most important indicator that's that's employee mobility.

918
00:58:25,160 --> 00:58:28,070
Yeah.

919
00:58:28,070 --> 00:58:34,070
There's specialized recruiters actually just doing Rust recruiting, which also speaks a little bit to maturity.

920
00:58:34,070 --> 00:58:38,710
And most companies that I know that post their jobs at the right places report,

921
00:58:38,710 --> 00:58:41,590
"Yeah, we're finding about 10 to 20 good applicants."

922
00:58:41,590 --> 00:58:43,470
The other thing is usually Rust jobs,

923
00:58:43,470 --> 00:58:46,230
and that's something that I hear from a lot of people that are hiring.

924
00:58:46,230 --> 00:58:49,070
and also seeing in our own hiring data,

925
00:58:49,070 --> 00:58:53,510
is the medium level of quality of the people who actually apply is pretty high.

926
00:58:53,510 --> 00:58:57,870
And who's complaining about I have 10 to 20 pretty good pre-screened candidates.

927
00:58:57,870 --> 00:59:00,832
rather than I have 200 with. Yeah. who would spot you.

928
00:59:00,840 --> 00:59:04,430
So actually, so if they they have to screen fewer candidates,

929
00:59:04,430 --> 00:59:11,272
but they have a better chance to actually hire someone because the average level is higher anyway. Yes. Okay.

930
00:59:11,272 --> 00:59:12,990
And so my last question is about the future.

931
00:59:12,990 --> 00:59:18,430
How do you envision the future of Rust in the safety-critical domain?

932
00:59:18,440 --> 00:59:22,470
Okay, I envision it pretty big.

933
00:59:24,790 --> 00:59:26,870
I honestly think Rust will become the next standard language for those areas.

934
00:59:26,880 --> 00:59:34,471
And the reason for that is memory safety will become a mandate in a lot of projects, and we're already seeing that.

935
00:59:34,480 --> 00:59:38,550
We're seeing requests for quotations being handed around that say,

936
00:59:38,550 --> 00:59:41,270
"Do only implement in a memory safe programming language."

937
00:59:41,280 --> 00:59:44,070
So, that's the one thing.

938
00:59:44,070 --> 00:59:48,190
So, and we're seeing like the buying side is far more interested in you need to have a.

939
00:59:48,190 --> 00:59:50,870
memory safety story.

940
00:59:50,870 --> 00:59:52,830
And we'll enter a space where, okay,

941
00:59:52,830 --> 00:59:56,350
I use a programming language where I need additional tooling, additional skilled engineers,

942
00:59:56,360 --> 00:59:58,510
and so on and so forth.

943
00:59:58,510 --> 01:00:01,351
Or I could just take one that actually fits that bill right away. Yeah.

944
01:00:01,360 --> 01:00:03,430
That is the one thing.

945
01:00:03,430 --> 01:00:09,950
The other thing I see though is there's a very good interchange between all of those languages,

946
01:00:09,950 --> 01:00:11,790
and C++ is still in the game, to be completely fair there.

947
01:00:11,800 --> 01:00:16,390
And they say it's like competition is business.

948
01:00:16,390 --> 01:00:18,470
So, that also applies to programming languages.

949
01:00:18,470 --> 01:00:20,950
So, it is very important that a Swift.

950
01:00:20,950 --> 01:00:25,750
and C++ are around because that also makes sure that the Rust team is not lazy.

951
01:00:25,750 --> 01:00:30,270
And I know, for example, we have very good relationship with the C++ committee.

952
01:00:30,280 --> 01:00:36,990
Or some. I wouldn't say like I I can't claim an official relationship with the C++ committee,

953
01:00:36,990 --> 01:00:42,510
but we've been talking for years about things, and I know everything. that is done in the C++ side, they will also look at what Rust does,

954
01:00:42,520 --> 01:00:44,870
and we obviously look at what C++ does.

955
01:00:44,880 --> 01:00:48,630
LLVM has just announced last week that they want to have a committee in.

956
01:00:48,630 --> 01:00:53,830
which they do safety information on the clang compiler, and put.

957
01:00:53,830 --> 01:00:55,870
that open source, very similar to how we do.

958
01:00:55,870 --> 01:00:59,150
There is a good cross-pollination between the two sides.

959
01:00:59,160 --> 01:01:02,870
As they say in Germany, there's music in it.

960
01:01:02,880 --> 01:01:06,750
And this is much more the space where I want to be,

961
01:01:06,750 --> 01:01:11,230
where people go and meet and say like, "Okay, we have three technologies here. and let's figure out how we make it better and better."

962
01:01:11,240 --> 01:01:17,310
Sounds like a situation where there is a healthy competition in terms of trying to improve all these.

963
01:01:17,310 --> 01:01:19,551
various languages and technologies. Yeah, exactly.

964
01:01:19,551 --> 01:01:22,230
And it's important.

965
01:01:22,230 --> 01:01:28,350
Like, we have literally millions and millions of lines of C and C++ code out there.

966
01:01:28,350 --> 01:01:30,830
It would be bad if we ended up saying, "Okay, this is done.

967
01:01:30,840 --> 01:01:32,631
No one cares about this." Like we.

968
01:01:32,631 --> 01:01:40,470
This is just structurally impossible, but it also means for a language like Rust going in and saying,

969
01:01:40,470 --> 01:01:43,390
"Okay, C is now how much from the '60s?"

970
01:01:43,400 --> 01:01:47,830
One of the oldest languages we still use in broad use.

971
01:01:47,830 --> 01:01:50,470
C++ is a little bit younger.

972
01:01:50,470 --> 01:01:52,070
It would also be like as engineers we.

973
01:01:52,070 --> 01:01:53,870
also need to say there's probably a lot of learning.

974
01:01:53,870 --> 01:01:55,910
that we can take from all of these years.

975
01:01:55,910 --> 01:01:59,670
and not recommit those mistakes and put that into a new product.

976
01:01:59,670 --> 01:02:03,230
And Rust is just to be clear, this is a debate that we very often have.

977
01:02:03,230 --> 01:02:06,390
Rust is an open source project that sees itself as a product.

978
01:02:06,400 --> 01:02:09,750
So this is something that needs to go into the hands of users.

979
01:02:09,750 --> 01:02:12,270
and needs to be designed with that mindset.

980
01:02:12,280 --> 01:02:16,830
And I actually think it's one of the big factors in its success.

981
01:02:16,830 --> 01:02:19,830
Like aside from all of the details, that mindset is of.

982
01:02:19,840 --> 01:02:22,030
The most important thing is the user.

983
01:02:22,040 --> 01:02:25,910
And this is something where we have quite a lot of good exchange.

984
01:02:25,910 --> 01:02:28,270
and I think we're in a good space in terms of programming languages.

985
01:02:28,270 --> 01:02:32,110
If you love programming languages, we're in a great space.

986
01:02:32,120 --> 01:02:34,111
Okay. Thanks, Florian.

987
01:02:34,111 --> 01:02:36,230
So now we have come close to the end of the episode.

988
01:02:36,230 --> 01:02:41,390
Is there anything that we missed that you'd like to add?

989
01:02:41,390 --> 01:02:43,990
No, I think we talked about how all programming languages are great in the end.

990
01:02:43,990 --> 01:02:47,350
I think that's a good ending.

991
